<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · period</p><h1 class="enc-title">Information Age</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Information Age is a historical period that began in the mid-20th century, marked by a rapid shift from the traditional industries of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/industrial-revolution">Industrial Revolution</a> to an economy centred on information technology. Its onset has been linked to the first working <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transistor">transistor</a>, invented in 1947 by John Bardeen and Walter Houser Brattain under William Shockley at Bell Labs, which led the way to more compact digital computers. The digital revolution converted technology from analog to digital format, so that identical copies could be made; a message was sent over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arpanet">ARPANET</a> in 1969, and in the 1980s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/compact-disc">compact discs</a> replaced vinyl records and cassette tapes.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Humans have made tools for counting and calculating since ancient times, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abacus">abacus</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrolabe">astrolabe</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slide-rule">slide rule</a> and mechanical calculators appeared in the 1600s. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-babbage">Charles Babbage</a> proposed a mechanical general-purpose computer, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/analytical-engine">Analytical Engine</a>, which was never successfully built. In the 1880s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herman-hollerith">Herman Hollerith</a> developed electromechanical tabulating devices using punch cards. In 1947 John Bardeen and Walter Houser Brattain, working under William Shockley at Bell Labs, invented the first working <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transistor">transistor</a>. In 1948 the mathematician Claude Shannon published A Mathematical Theory of Communication, generally credited with laying the foundations of digitalisation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The digital revolution turned technology from analog to digital format, which made copies identical to the original. During the 1980s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/compact-disc">compact discs</a> gradually replaced vinyl records and cassettes. A message was sent over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arpanet">ARPANET</a> in 1969, and its protocols let separate networks be joined into a network of networks. Tim Berners-Lee invented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-wide-web">World Wide Web</a> in 1989. Moore's law, formulated around 1965, holds that the number of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transistor">transistors</a> in a dense <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/integrated-circuit">integrated circuit</a> doubles about every two years. The world's capacity to store information grew from 2.6 exabytes in 1986 to 295 exabytes in 2007.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Writers compare the Information Age with the Neolithic Age, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution</a> and the Industrial Age, which brought irreversible changes to daily life but took hundreds or thousands of years, whereas the Information Age reached all parts of the globe in a few years. Activities involving information existed in every society and grew into institutions such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/platonic-academy">Platonic Academy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/library-of-alexandria">Library of Alexandria</a>. Economists treat information as a factor of production alongside capital, labour and land, and as a good sold in the market. Some writers argue that advances in artificial intelligence and biotechnology have begun a Fourth Industrial Revolution.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Information Age” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
